---
scope: src/tools/cleanup
description: Guarded eraser that removes a closed module's pipeline ephemerals only when every file it would remove is fully checked, refusing with the exact items still open.
intent: >
  cleanup is the eraser that pairs with scaffold's pen. When a module's
  pipeline cycle closes, its plan, todo list, and brief have served their
  purpose and should disappear, leaving code and intent specs as the
  durable record. A raw shell delete is blocked by safety tooling and
  enforces nothing, so this tool holds the safety rules in code: it removes
  ephemerals only when every file it would remove proves its cycle closed,
  and otherwise refuses with exactly what remains. A fully checked todo
  list is its own proof, even with no plan beside it.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Given a module, the tool removes only that module's plan, todo list, and brief. Intent specs, research specs, code, the session log, and every other file survive every call untouched.
    - Where a plan exists, removal proceeds only when the plan has every item checked and any todo list present is fully checked; a missing todo list never blocks. The opt-in that accepts a plan with no checkboxes relaxes that one check and nothing else.
    - Where no plan exists, the tool removes what is present once every file it would remove is fully checked, so a finished todo list written beside code planned in another module is never stranded. A brief is removed only together with its plan, because a brief alone proves nothing closed.
    - Every precondition, including that the path is an existing folder inside the project holding ephemerals, is settled before anything is removed, so a call cleans completely or removes nothing. A refusal names each unchecked item by file and line, so the caller knows exactly what work remains.
    - Because the real call is already safe, an agent closing a module calls it directly with no preview step. An opt-in dry run exists for inspection only, changes nothing, and never disagrees with what the real call would do against the same state.
    - Cleanup is a single cross-platform action a user can permit by name, so closing a module never requires a raw shell delete or a broad delete permission.
  undesired:
    - A call that removes, rewrites, or even reads the session log, an intent spec, a research spec, code, or any file that is not a pipeline ephemeral of the named module, however the module was named. Nor does any call serve as a listing of what a folder holds.
    - Removal that proceeds with an unchecked item in any file it would remove, that removes a brief whose plan is absent, or that lets the empty-plan opt-in waive anything beyond a plan with no checkboxes.
    - A fully checked todo list refused only because no plan sits beside it, leaving a manual shell delete as the only way out.
    - A half-cleaned module, with some ephemerals removed and others left behind, or a call against a path outside the project, a missing folder, or a folder with no ephemerals that reports success.
    - A dry run that predicts a different result than the real call, or a cleanup flow that treats a dry run as a required step before the real call.
    - A refusal that only says it refused, without naming the unchecked items or the file holding them.
---
